WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.789 --> 00:00:05.230
Business is bad for carpenter Nazir Ahmad
and his employee Abdul Satar.

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:08.640
Along with international military units leaving
Afghanistan,

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.080
many foreign aid workers have also left.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:14.260
Since those departures, Ahmad hasn't had many
orders.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:20.160
On top of that, the overall security situation
in Kabul has worsened significantly.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:25.340
"I am always scared.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:30.040
When I leave the house in the morning I often
ask myself if I'll come home alive.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:35.080
I'm scared for my wife and my children - they
could also be killed by a bomb.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:36.910
We are constantly in danger."

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.980
Kabul looks like a fortress now.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:45.840
Police checkpoints are everywhere.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:51.660
Politicians, warlords, ambassadors and aid
organizations barricade themselves behind

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.200
massive concrete walls designed to withstand
bomb attacks.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:59.480
They often hire private security firms to
patrol their compounds.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:05.540
Very few want to rely on the city's poorly
trained and often corrupt police officers.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:12.300
"Kabul has been the target of seven major
attacks so far this year.

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:16.110
That’s more than at any time since the intervention
started.

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:21.530
Even with a modest increase in military assistance
by the United States or its partners, experts

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:25.340
predict that the situation will only get worse."

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.700
People in Kabul are extremely frustrated about
the situation.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:37.840
Many blame their own government for not being
able to disarm the warlords and their militias.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.440
"Our government can't do anything - how are
they supposed to bring peace?"

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:53.440
"Nobody can help Afghanistan more than its
own people -- so they have to stand up

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:55.220
and fight for Afghanistan."

00:01:55.580 --> 00:02:00.160
We hear again and again that an Afghan solution
would also require reconciliation

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:01.220
with the Taliban.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:02.320
But it's not that easy.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:07.720
Mohammed Qasim Halimi worked for the Taliban
regime of Mullah Omar for years.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:11.000
Now he works for the new government's religious
council -

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.540
yet still keeps in contact with the Taliban.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:20.480
"I can say that 80 percent of the Taliban
want to negotiate with the Afghan government.

00:02:20.489 --> 00:02:25.989
They want to solve the problem, they want
to stop the war -- but some of them

00:02:25.989 --> 00:02:31.920
don’t want to negotiate, and they are actually
the ones who have the power."

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:36.049
We also asked carpenter Nazir Ahmad why he
thinks the government hasn't been able to

00:02:36.049 --> 00:02:37.620
unite the country.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:41.010
Like many frustrated Afghans, he doesn't have
an answer.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:44.520
But he's sure that building up military might
is NOT the solution.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:49.260
"I don't think that more international troops
here would accomplish anything.

00:02:49.269 --> 00:02:52.300
What did they achieve when 130 thousand soldiers
were here?

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:55.480
And if they send a couple of thousand over
now - so what?"

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:59.989
Ahmad hopes that the international community,
in cooperation with the Afghan government,

00:02:59.989 --> 00:03:01.689
will create more jobs.

00:03:01.689 --> 00:03:05.680
That would help to prevent radical groups
from taking advantage of disaffected youth.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:11.460
Ahmad says better job prospects would
bring more security to this war-torn land.

